Apple is working on a high-end <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a>, and it's nearly ready to launch. It will have several firsts in a Mac, including an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> display and a touchscreen.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/MacBook-Pro-Dynamic-Island-multicolor.jpg" alt="" width="2100" height="1181" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1071828" /><br/>
<h2 id="design">Design</h2><br/>
Apple hasn't redesigned the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; since the launch of the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/m1-pro/">M1 Pro</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/m1-max/">M1 Max</a> models in 2021, so it's due for a new look. The new &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; is expected to feature some minor design changes. There's only so much you can do in a laptop form factor, but rumors say it will be lighter and possibly a bit thinner than before, which suggests some tweaks to the casing.<br/>
<br/>
Apple plans to get rid of the notch, replacing it with an iPhone-style <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/dynamic-island/">Dynamic Island</a>. A &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; could better unify the way <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI behaves across the iPhone and the Mac with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macos-27/">macOS Golden Gate</a>, and it will take up less space on the display.<br/>
<br/>
We haven't heard rumors of keyboard or trackpad changes, and neither needs an overhaul.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="size_options">Size Options</h2><br/>
The &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; will be available in both 14-inch and 16-inch size options. It's not yet clear if it will replace the current models or be sold as a higher-end option alongside them.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="display">Display</h2><br/>
Apple's upcoming &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; will be the first with an &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display. &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display technology is already used for the iPhone, Apple Watch, and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-pro/">iPad Pro</a>, but it has taken time for larger-sized &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; screens to come down in price.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;OLED&zwnj; will be an upgrade over the current mini-LED display technology in most cases, bringing richer colors and a higher contrast ratio with true blacks. In an &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display, individual pixels turn off instead of dimming when not in use, so there's less blooming. &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; displays tend to have better HDR than mini-LED, but sometimes don't match the mini-LED brightness levels.<br/>
<br/>
Rumors indicate Apple is using rigid &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panels with oxide TFT backplanes and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/01/16/apples-oled-macbook-pro-panel-production/">advanced tandem OLED structures</a>. Tandem &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panels are used in the &zwnj;iPad Pro&zwnj; and have a longer lifespan with less burn-in than single-stack &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; panels.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="touchscreen">Touchscreen</h2><br/>
Along with &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; technology, the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; is expected to have the first touchscreen display on a Mac. Users will be able to use their fingers to interact with the Mac's display, similar to an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Touchscreen capabilities will be used alongside the trackpad and keyboard, suggesting Apple sees touch as supplementary instead of a primary control method. Apple is rumored to be adapting &zwnj;macOS Golden Gate&zwnj; for touch input.<br/>
<br/>
Apple plans to add a reinforced hinge to the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;'s display so that it doesn't wobble when it's tapped.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="touchscreen_hints_in_macos_27">Touchscreen Hints in macOS 27</h3><br/>
There are features in &zwnj;macOS Golden Gate&zwnj; that <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/10/macos-27-hints-at-macbook-ultra/">hint at a future touchscreen Mac</a>. Apple added direct touch input to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/sidecar/">Sidecar</a> so users can tap and interact with macOS elements when using the &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; as a Mac display.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;macOS Golden Gate&zwnj; supports an iPhone-style pull-to-refresh option, and it can be used across apps like Safari, Mail, News, Podcasts, and Calendar. A pull-to-refresh option makes the most sense on a touch display.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="chips">Chips</h2><br/>
The current high-end &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models are equipped with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/26/oled-macbook-stick-with-m5-pro-and-m5-max/">M5 Pro and M5 Max chips</a>, and Apple plans to use the same chips for the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
Apple already <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/25/apple-announces-2026-mac-mini/">introduced an M6 chip in the latest Mac mini</a>, and it's expected to appear in a low-end &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; and an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> on October 28, but <em>Bloomberg</em> says Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/m6-macbook-pro-2026/">isn't going to release</a> any other chips in the M6 series. Apple's chip plans changed earlier this year, and the company is now accelerating the launch of the M7 series. The M7 chips feature major neural-processing improvements that will meet demand for machines better able to handle AI tasks.<br/>
<br/>
With no M6 Pro or M6 Max planned, Apple will need to use the M5 Pro and M5 Max so it doesn't have to wait for the M7 series before debuting the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;. A second-generation &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; will use the M7 Pro and M7 Max chips, with that machine planned for late 2027.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="naming">Naming</h2><br/>
There were rumors that the &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; model would be called the "<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">MacBook Ultra</a>," but that's apparently not happening. Apple's product unveiling has the tagline "Pro and tell," making it clear the new device will keep the Pro name.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="pricing">Pricing</h2><br/>
Apple's &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; is expected to be a premium product, with a price tag higher than current &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models. Apple <a href="https://macrumors.com/2026/06/25/apple-just-increased-prices">raised the prices of all Macs</a> in June 2026, so the &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; could be even more expensive. &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; pricing went up a minimum of &#36;300 as part of the June price hikes.<br/>
<br/>
The higher-end 14-inch &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; with M5 Pro chip starts at &#36;2,499 after the price increase, while the 16-inch M5 Pro &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; starts at &#36;2,999. The M5 Max models start at &#36;4,099 for the 14-inch model and &#36;4,399 for the 16-inch model.<br/>
<br/>
<h2 id="launch_date">Launch Date</h2><br/>
Apple will introduce new Macs <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/19/apple-october-28-product-launch/">on Wednesday, October 28</a> as part of a "Pro and tell" launch. There won't be a livestreamed event, but some members of the media have been invited to Los Angeles to try out the new devices.<br/>
